- Denis GOUNELLE, tell us our story with the Amiga.

  I discovered the Amiga in 1988, thanks to an university friend who had
  an A2000. I bought an A500 one year later. When I started to work, I
  switched to an A3000 as soon as I had the money for (in 1992).

- On which Amiga model do you work ?
  Does this computer fits your needs ?

  So I have an A3000, on which I have just added a 68060 accelerator board
  and a PicassoII graphic board. I have 24 Mb of memory, a 160 Mb hard-drive
  (filled at about 40% of its capacity), a modem, a CD-ROM drive, etc...
  I will let you guess if this configuration is suitable or not :-)

- Are you planning to add harware extensions to your Amiga, or to get another
  Amiga model ?

  Oh no... it's fine now :-) I may buy a 17 inches monitor next year, but
  I have no other plan for the moment.

- Do you have another computer at home ?
  If no, do you plan to buy a PC or a Mac ?

  Both answer will be negatives. I love my A3000, and it fits my needs
  perfectly for the moment... especially since I've offered him a new
  youht !

- Which programming tools do you use ?

  I'm using the SAS/C compiler since I have my A3000, without any special
  tool other that my text editor.

- A lot of people thought that you had gave up the Amiga. What happened
  actually ?

  Well, just some intensive laziness :-) More seriously, my job is quite
  engrossing and stressful, so I don't have the courage anymore to spend
  my evenings and my week-ends to make programs on my Amiga. On the other
  hand, I spend quite a lot of time working on my Web site. You might
  also consider that I have just released updates of ADoc, AFile, and
  of course ABackup. But I admit these updates didn't required a huge
  amount of work.

- Is a PPC version of ABackup planned ?

  As soon as some generous soul offers me a PPC board and the associated
  compiler.

- Do you think ABackup is still usefull, now that removable disk systems
  are getting cheaper ?

  There is still a lot of people who only have a floppy disk system, to
  backup their hard-drive. So I think (and I hope !) that ABackup is still
  usefull. Even for people owning a removable disk system, I think a backup
  is faster with ABackup than with a classic copy operation, or than with
  some archiver like LhA.

- Did you get a lot of registration from France ? And from foreign countries ?

  About 1/3 of the registrations come from France, and 2/3 from abroad.
  The first foreign country is Germany, by far.

- Do you think that it is possible to make a living with shareware programs,
  in France ?

  No. Unless you make an indispensable program, with a very fine programming.
  But then, why not release it as a commercial program ?

- It seems that people register themselves more easily when they can do it
  on the net, with a credit card. What do you think of this ? Will it be
  possible to register to ABackup from your Web site ?

  No. I am not a commercial company.
  But of course, email enables me to provide a technical support for the
  users of my programs, and my Web site allow to give some informations
  about upcoming versions.

- Do you think that the PPC is a good choice for the Amiga ?

  I am not a micro-processor specialist, but I think this is a good choice.
  Better than few widespread processors like the ARM, the HP RISC (which was
  an option for a while) or the R4000 from MIPS, as a few examples.

- What do you think the Amiga is lacking, in order to be more up to date
  and to better fit the users needs ?

  There are a few things that the Amiga is lacking, when you think about
  what is offering a well-knowned company which name is starting by "M".
  The worst lack, to my mind, is the impossibility to merge several types
  of documents in a single one (I mean, for example, to include a
  spreadsheet into a word processor document).
  I would also like to see a much more "Object Oriented" operating system.
  Datatypes are a step in this direction, but I find them very difficult
  to use for the programmer. I would like to find some "super-datatypes",
  which would allow any program to read and write any file format it
  handles (picture, text, sound, etc...) in a transparent way, for the
  programmer as well as for the user.

- We are (almost) at the beginning of 1998, so it's the time for everyone
  to make predictions about the Amiga future. Tell us what is your vision.

  I think that there will always be a lot of people allergic to the magic
  world of the PC and to Microsoft's softwares. I hope the Amiga will soon
  be, once again, the best solution to escape from this hell.

- Do you think that the Amiga is still usuable for familly and professional
  usages ?

  Yes, even if on the family side, beginners may have some problems to find
  someone able to help them. On the professional side, the Amiga is not up
  to the job for some specific usages (large server for instance).

- Your Web site deals quite a lot of cats. How did you get interested by
  them ? Do cats have an influence on your programming work, on your live ?

  I don't know how I get into cats. I'm just someone who love cats, that's
  all. Cats seems to like me too, maybe it's just because we share the same
  temperament. A small kitty has some influence on my programming work, during
  a few days : between two sleeps on my bed, just beside me, she used to find
  quite funny to see my hands moving on the keyboard, so she jumped on my knees
  and to try to catch them.

- Thanks for having taken the time to answer to these questions. May we remind that
  Denis GOUNELLE can be contacted by email (denis.gounelle@wanadoo.fr,) and that
  you can visit his Web site at "http://perso.wanadoo.fr/denis.gounelle/index.html".

